## Notes on Arguments and Attributes

- `environment_id`: Forces a new kubeconfig.
- `expiry_seconds`: Forces a new kubeconfig.
- `namespaces`: Forces a new kubeconfig.
- `include_secrets`: Defaults to `false`. Forces a new kubeconfig.
- `description`: Forces a new kubeconfig.

## Import

Scoped kubeconfigs can be imported with `<environment_id>:<scoped_kubeconfig_id>`:

```shell
terraform import portainer_kubernetes_scoped_kubeconfig.ci 4:17
```

The import reads `namespaces`, `include_secrets`, `description` and `expiry_seconds` back, so a configuration that matches does not plan a replacement (for a kubeconfig issued without `namespaces`, the covered namespaces are imported; set `namespaces` to them). The credential itself cannot be recovered: `kubeconfig` stays empty.

If `namespaces` is left unset or reordered after an import, only the state is updated, with a warning when unset; use `terraform apply -replace` to reissue. This applies to the first plan after the import only: once that plan has been applied (even with nothing to change), a later change of `namespaces` issues a new kubeconfig as usual. With an `import` block, which plans in the same run, it also applies to the run after it.

See also: the [`portainer_kubernetes_scoped_kubeconfig` ephemeral resource](../ephemeral-resources/kubernetes_scoped_kubeconfig.md) keeps the secret out of the plan and the state (Terraform >= 1.10, OpenTofu >= 1.11).
